<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

You finished the support-desk domain in lessons 1 to 9 (forked from the upstream tutorial): `Tutorial.Support.Ticket` and `Tutorial.Support.Representative`, now carrying actions, relationships, an aggregate and calculations. A LiveView is just one more **caller of actions**. It gets no special access: it reads through a read action, as an actor, and the resource's policies decide what it sees. That is the whole lesson; the rest is LiveView mechanics done properly.

Here is the domain, with one addition: a `policies` block, so the actor matters. Note the `Ticket` below is a trimmed redefinition of the upstream one, not a superset: `description` and `updated_at` are dropped, `created_at` is spelled `inserted_at`, and an `owner_id` replaces the representative link. These lessons re-derive from a small surface, and nothing here depends on what upstream carried.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4" style="background-color: #fff4e5; color: #61758a;">

> **Edit the original cell, never a new one.** From here on, lessons define modules in cells. To change a module — add an action, a validation, a `pub_sub` block — edit the cell that defined it and re-run that cell. Defining the same module in a *second* cell is a compile error on current Elixir (`module Tutorial.Support.Ticket is already defined`), and purging the code first does not help.

</div>

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Support,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    authorizers: [Ash.Policy.Authorizer]

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :subject, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :status, :atom, constraints: [one_of: [:open, :closed]], default: :open, public?: true
    attribute :owner_id, :uuid, public?: true
    create_timestamp :inserted_at, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]
    create :open, accept: [:subject, :owner_id]
    update :close, change: set_attribute(:status, :closed)

    read :open_tickets do
      filter expr(status == :open)
      pagination keyset?: true, default_limit: 20, required?: false
    end
  end

  policies do
    policy action_type(:read) do
      authorize_if expr(owner_id == ^actor(:id))
    end

    policy action_type([:create, :update]) do
      authorize_if actor_present()
    end
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.Support do
  use Ash.Domain

  resources do
    resource Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
      define :open_ticket, action: :open, args: [:subject]
      define :list_open, action: :open_tickets
      define :close_ticket, action: :close
    end
  end
end
```

## Actors, not filters

The LiveView never writes `where owner_id == current_user.id`. That rule lives in the policy, so it applies to every caller. Create two actors and a few tickets, then list as each.

```elixir
alice = %{id: Ash.UUID.generate()}
bob = %{id: Ash.UUID.generate()}

for s <- ["Printer on fire", "VPN down"],
    do: Tutorial.Support.open_ticket!(s, %{owner_id: alice.id}, actor: alice)

Tutorial.Support.open_ticket!("Bob's laptop", %{owner_id: bob.id}, actor: bob)
:ok
```

List open tickets as `alice`, then as `bob`, using the code interface.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  Tutorial.Support.list_open!(actor: alice) |> Enum.map(& &1.subject)
  Tutorial.Support.list_open!(actor: bob) |> Enum.map(& &1.subject)
  ```

  Same action, different rows. Reads filter rather than raise: a policy on a read becomes a filter.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** Same action, two actors, two different lists — the policy filtered, and nothing raised.

**Look around.** The policy lives in the resource; the LiveView and the notebook are just two callers who happened to ask as different people. That symmetry is the whole architecture — everything from lesson 15 on leans on it.

## The LiveView rules

Three rules you will be held to (they are iron laws; note that lesson 14's judge has no detector for the second one — it is behavioral, so your reviewer catches it instead):

1. **No queries in `mount/3`.** `mount` runs twice: once for the static render, once on connect. Load in `handle_params/3`, which also runs when the URL changes.
2. **Collections are streams.** `stream/3` keeps the list on the client, not in server memory.
3. **The actor comes from the socket**, set by `on_mount` auth in a real app. A cell's `Process.get/1` cannot cross the process boundary — `mount/3` runs in the LiveView's *own* process, so anything your notebook process put in the process dictionary is simply not there. Here we bake the actor in as a module attribute at compile time.

```elixir
defmodule TicketsLive do
  use Phoenix.LiveView

  @demo_actor alice

  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do
    # static: no queries here
    {:ok, assign(socket, current_user: @demo_actor)}
  end

  def handle_params(_params, _uri, socket) do
    tickets = Tutorial.Support.list_open!(actor: socket.assigns.current_user)
    {:noreply, stream(socket, :tickets, tickets, reset: true)}
  end

  def render(assigns) do
    ~H"""
    <h1>Open tickets</h1>
    <ul id="tickets" phx-update="stream">
      <li :for={{dom_id, t} <- @streams.tickets} id={dom_id}>{t.subject}</li>
    </ul>
    """
  end
end
```

`list_open!` returns a **list**. `open_tickets` declares pagination, so you may *ask* for a page — `list_open!(actor: alice, page: [limit: 20])` returns an `Ash.Page.Keyset` struct whose `.results` holds the records — but unless you pass `page:`, you get the plain list, which is what the LiveView above streams. Run it:

```elixir
PhoenixPlayground.start(live: TicketsLive, port: 4010, open_browser: false)
```

Open http://localhost:4010. You should see only Alice's tickets. The `Representative` sits this part out: from lesson 14 on you work inside a real app, where the people who work the desk are the app's own users — and it is one of them, not a notebook struct, who proposes refunds in lesson 15.

One thing to file away while you are here: Alice's first seeded ticket is a printer on fire. The customer behind that printer — and the refund she is owed — is what the rest of this series is about.

## Exercise: add a status filter from the URL

Add a `?status=closed` URL parameter. Do **not** filter in the LiveView. Add an argument to a read action and let the action filter.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  Add to the resource:

  ```elixir
  read :by_status do
    argument :status, :atom, constraints: [one_of: [:open, :closed]], default: :open
    filter expr(status == ^arg(:status))
  end
  ```

  and to the domain `define :list_by_status, action: :by_status, args: [:status]`. Then in `handle_params/3`:

  ```elixir
  status = Map.get(params, "status", "open")
  tickets = Tutorial.Support.list_by_status!(status, actor: socket.assigns.current_user)
  ```

  Pass the string. The action's argument type casts and constrains it, so you never call `String.to_atom/1` on user input.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** `?status=closed` now works through the action, and a status the constraint does not know dies at the argument — never at `String.to_atom/1`.

